{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080532,"nonce":"AducEER7sb5rbUSv6vzl5r3oXXo=","title":"`lint-reachability` fails a retirement for doing exactly what a retirement is, because removing a field's last reader mints a new finding and the baseline may only shrink","message":"**What is wrong.** `lintreach` reports a struct field with zero production reads as `noread` and a field with zero production writes as `nowrite` (`tools/lintreach/main.go:23`). Retiring a feature in place — the pattern this repo has chosen three times now — deletes the readers and keeps the field, which is precisely how a `noread` finding is created. The tool's contract then makes that finding fatal: `main.go:122-125` — *\"A finding NOT on the list fails immediately, so the debt can only shrink, and a baseline line that matches nothing is itself an error, so a paid debt cannot keep its exemption.\"* A retirement therefore cannot be landed green without a `//oto:reachable-ok` on every field it deafens, and the escape hatch has its own trap: `:117-119` — *\"The reason is mandatory and a suppression that matches nothing is an error, so the escape hatch cannot rot\"* — so the same marker becomes an error the day the field is finally deleted or re-read.\n\n**The demonstration.** A run on this branch, right now, reports three new findings, and one of them is the flap retirement's own footprint:\n\n    internal/notification/domain/mode.go:178: noread:…domain.PlanInput.Flapping\n        field is never read in production — the value goes nowhere\n\nThat field is not an accident and not debt. `mode.go:160-164` states the intent in the imperative: *\"⛔ THE FIELD SURVIVES ON PURPOSE AND MUST NOT ACQUIRE A NEW MEANING. `notification/service.notify` still sets it from the snapshot (`service/notify.go:113`), `PlanFor` ignores it, and a caller reading a plan gets the same modes whether it is true or false. Giving it a second job would put a damper back at the layer that cannot account for its own silence.\"* Written, argued, deliberate — and a build failure, because `PlanFor` stopped reading it.\n\nThe mechanism, read out of `main.go`: `findings()` produces the list; `applySuppressions` removes anything carrying a marker; `:199-207` splits the rest into `fresh` (not in `baseline.txt`) and `known` (in it); `:221-225` collects baseline keys nothing matched as `stale`; `:246-262` exits non-zero if either `fresh` or `stale` is non-empty. There is no third state. A field cannot be \"retired, reader gone on purpose, not debt, not suppressed\" — it is a new finding, and `just lint-reachability` (`justfile:345-346`, in `ci` at `:374`) is red.\n\n**Why it matters.** The gate's purpose is right and worth keeping: a field nothing reads is usually a bug, and catching it at CI is the whole value. The cost is that it prices retire-in-place at one suppression comment per field plus a follow-up to remove each comment later, and it charges that price at the moment a change is largest — a retirement touching many fields at once. The predictable response is the wrong one: a maintainer under time pressure either deletes the field (which is the thing the retirement bargain deliberately declined, because a decoder or a stored row may still need it) or writes a `reachable-ok` reason that is not true.\n\nThere is a second, quieter cost. `reachable-ok` means *\"genuinely reachable by a route this analyzer cannot see\"* (`:246-249`'s failure message). A retired field is the opposite: it is genuinely unreachable and meant to be. Using the marker for it makes every future `reachable-ok` in the tree ambiguous between \"the analyzer is blind here\" and \"this is retired\", which are opposite claims about the same code.\n\n**Where.** `tools/lintreach/main.go:23` (what `noread`/`nowrite` mean), `:113-125` (the suppression-and-baseline contract, including \"the debt can only shrink\" and \"a suppression that matches nothing is an error\"), `:167-172` (the flags: `-v`, `-report`, `-write-baseline`), `:199-207` (fresh vs known), `:221-230` (`stale`, and `unusedMarkers`), `:246-262` (the two failure messages and `os.Exit(1)`), `:1230-1251` (`loadBaseline`) · `justfile:345-346` and `:374` (`lint-reachability`, and its place in `ci`) · `internal/notification/domain/mode.go:160-178` (`PlanInput.Flapping` — a live example of the collision) · `internal/alerts/service/deps.go:296-304` and `internal/alerts/service/sweep.go:740-741` (two more retire-in-place sites whose fields are next).\n\n**Scope.** medium. The mechanism is fifty lines; the design question is what a retired declaration is called and how the tool tells it apart from debt and from blindness.\n\n**Done when.** Retiring a feature in place does not require a `reachable-ok` reason that claims the opposite of what is true. `lintreach` distinguishes \"unreachable and that is the point\" from \"unreachable and somebody should look\" — whether by a distinct marker (`//oto:retired \u003creason\u003e`), by a baseline section that admits new entries under review rather than only shrinking, or by another mechanism — and whichever is chosen, the marker on `PlanInput.Flapping` reads as the deliberate retirement `mode.go:160` describes. `reachable-ok` keeps its single meaning: the analyzer cannot see a route that exists. The rule that a stale entry is an error survives, because it is what stops the exemption rotting, and it must keep applying to whatever the new state is called.","files":null}]}